```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 9,
                      fig.height = 7, dpi = 110, out.width = "100%")
options(rgeopl.cache_dir = "D:/Apps/rgeopl/.cache-vignette")
```

The Forest Data Bank publishes the State Forests' administrative hierarchy and
its subareas. None of it needs a VPN.

```{r}
library(rgeopl)
```

## The hierarchy, and the key that runs through it

Four levels, and every object at every level carries the same key: the forest
address.

```{r}
parse_forest_address(c("04",
                       "04-02",
                       "04-02-2-11",
                       "04-02-2-11-165   -a   -00"))
```

Twenty-five characters, seven fixed-width fields, and the higher levels leave
the lower ones blank rather than omitting them -- so the positions never move
and a truncated address is still a valid one. That is what makes the address
usable as a search term.

## Listing the country

The name tables are small and are fetched without geometry, which keeps them
quick enough to work with interactively.

```{r}
bdl_overview()
```

```{r}
inspectorates <- bdl_catalogue("inspectorate")
ranges <- bdl_catalogue("range")
head(ranges, 4)
```

Structure falls out of that directly:

```{r}
per_inspectorate <- as.integer(table(paste(ranges$directorate_cd, ranges$inspectorate_cd)))
summary(per_inspectorate)

sort(table(ranges$inspectorate_name), decreasing = TRUE)[1:6]
```

```{r national-structure, fig.height = 5}
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4.2, 8.5, 3, 1))
ov <- bdl_overview(); ov <- ov[ov$directorate_cd != "--", ]
o <- ov[order(ov$ranges), ]
barplot(o$ranges, names.arg = o$directorate_name, horiz = TRUE, las = 1,
        cex.names = 0.75, col = "#3f7f5f", border = NA,
        xlab = "forest ranges", main = "Ranges per directorate")
par(mar = c(4.2, 4.2, 3, 1))
hist(per_inspectorate, breaks = seq(0.5, max(per_inspectorate) + 0.5, 1),
     col = "#7fb3a0", border = "white", xlab = "ranges in an inspectorate",
     ylab = "inspectorates", main = "How big is an inspectorate?")
abline(v = mean(per_inspectorate), lty = 2, lwd = 2)
par(op)
```

A warning about the `year` column while it is in view: it is the **edition
stamp of the BDL release**, identical across the whole country, not the year a
unit's management plan was revised. It reads 2026 for all 429 inspectorates. BDL
publishes the current state and nothing else -- there is no archive here, and no
field to tell old data from new. That is the one respect in which it is poorer
than the GUGiK indexes, where every vintage stays available and queryable.

## Finding a unit

By name, ignoring case and Polish diacritics:

```{r}
metkow <- bdl_unit(inspectorate = "Chrzanow", range = "Metkow")
sf::st_drop_geometry(metkow)
```

Or by address, at whatever depth you have. The most specific level you give is
the one you get back:

```{r}
bdl_by_address("04-02-2")$range_name
```

Below the forest range there is no index to query directly, so the range is
resolved first and its subareas filtered locally -- which keeps the request
bounded at hundreds of features rather than hundreds of thousands.

```{r}
compartment <- bdl_by_address("04-02-2-11-165")
sf::st_drop_geometry(compartment)[, c("adr_for", "area_ha", "species",
                                "species_age", "site_type")]
```

Wrong names and wrong compartments are answered with what does exist:

```{r error = TRUE}
bdl_unit(inspectorate = "Bircza", range = "Kopysno")
```

## Subareas over an area

```{r}
aoi <- as_aoi(sf::st_read(rgeopl_example("gleboczek_aoi.shp"), quiet = TRUE))
subareas <- bdl_subareas(aoi)
```

```{r}
d <- sf::st_drop_geometry(subareas)
sp <- aggregate(area_ha ~ species, data = d[!is.na(d$species), ], FUN = sum)
head(sp[order(-sp$area_ha), ], 6)
```

Compartments are not published as vectors at all -- they exist only as a WMS picture.
`bdl_compartments()` derives them by dissolving subareas on the compartment field of the
address, which is also self-consistent by construction:

```{r}
compartments <- bdl_compartments(aoi, quiet = TRUE)
head(sf::st_drop_geometry(compartments)[, c("inspectorate_name", "range_name", "compartment",
                                      "subareas", "area_ha", "area_geom_ha")], 5)
```

Two area columns, deliberately apart: `area_ha` is what the management plan
records, `area_geom_ha` is what the polygons enclose. They differ by around a
percent, and merging them would hide that.

## The trap: an address is not a polygon

A forest range's outline and the set of subareas addressed to it are not
the same thing.

```{r}
turnica <- bdl_by_address("04-02-2-11", quiet = TRUE)

by_polygon <- bdl_subareas(turnica, quiet = TRUE)
by_address <- bdl_subareas(turnica, within_aoi = FALSE, quiet = TRUE)
by_address <- subset(by_address, directorate_cd == "04" & inspectorate_cd == "02" &
                                 obreb_cd == "2" & range_cd == "11")

data.frame(
  selection = c("addressed to the range", "inside its polygon"),
  subareas = c(nrow(by_address), nrow(by_polygon)),
  area_ha = round(c(sum(by_address$area_ha, na.rm = TRUE),
                    sum(by_polygon$area_ha, na.rm = TRUE)), 1)
)
```

Fifty subareas lying inside Turnica's outline belong, by address, to four
neighbouring ranges. Taking the outline as the definition of the unit's
contents overstates its area by 36%.

Both readings are available on purpose. `bdl_subareas(aoi)` filters by geometry,
because "what is on this ground" is a spatial question. `bdl_by_address()`
filters by address, because "what belongs to this unit" is an administrative
one. Pick the one that matches the question.

```{r turnica-compartment}
plot_units(compartment, label = "subarea", context = by_address, fill = "#e8a33d",
           border = "black", main = "Compartment 165 among the subareas of Turnica")
```

## What is protected here

The Forest Data Bank draws conservation areas on its maps and publishes none
of them as data: its whole public service is seven layers of administrative
units and subareas. The register belongs to GDOŚ, and `protected_areas()` goes
there for it.

```{r}
warmia <- as_aoi(c(21.05, 53.80), buffer = 3000)
protected <- protected_areas(warmia, quiet = TRUE)

sf::st_drop_geometry(protected)[, c("type", "name", "code")]
```

Six features from four registers. `code` is the Natura 2000 site code and is
empty elsewhere, because only Natura 2000 has one. Ask for one register, or
for both directives at once:

```{r}
protected_areas(warmia, type = "natura2000", quiet = TRUE)$name
```

### Which stands lie under it

This is the question the two halves of this vignette meet on: the subareas
come from BDL, the protection from GDOŚ, and the answer is an intersection.

```{r}
stands <- bdl_subareas(warmia, quiet = TRUE)

share <- function(type) {
  g <- sf::st_union(sf::st_geometry(protected[protected$type == type, ]))
  lengths(sf::st_intersects(stands, g)) > 0
}

registers <- unique(protected$type)

data.frame(
  register = registers,
  stands = vapply(registers, function(t) sum(share(t)), integer(1)),
  of = nrow(stands),
  row.names = NULL
)
```

Most of these overlap hardly anything -- seven stands in the birds directive
site, two in the habitats one -- while the landscape complexes cover nearly
three quarters of the area. That spread is the point: "is this protected" has
no single answer, and which register you mean changes it by two orders of
magnitude.

```{r warmia-protected, fig.height = 6}
in_complex <- share("landscape_complexes")

plot(sf::st_geometry(stands), col = ifelse(in_complex, "#a8d5ba", "grey92"),
     border = "grey60", lwd = 0.4, main = "")
plot(sf::st_geometry(protected[protected$type == "landscape_complexes", ]),
     border = "#c1440e", lwd = 2, add = TRUE)
```

One thing to know before summing areas: a park and its buffer zone are separate
features under the same name. `ParkiNarodowe` returns 46 rows for Poland's 23
national parks, exactly 23 of them buffer zones, so counting or measuring
without looking counts the same ground twice.

## What is not here

- **No archive**, and no vintage field, as above.
- **No attribute filtering server-side.** `forest_range_name=Metkow` returns
  zero features whatever the spelling, which is why lookups go through a cached
  name table instead.
- **No names on the subareas.** They carry codes only, so the directorate,
  inspectorate and range names are joined in from the code tables.
